\section{Which policies can be undone}{

The five policies are not five parallel options: they are a ladder of
information loss, and how far down it you step is invisible until you try to
put the emoji back after the model call.\tabular{llll}{
   \code{policy} \tab \code{"great <U+1F600> work"} becomes \tab Restorable with \code{\link[=text_to_emoji]{text_to_emoji()}}? \tab What is lost \cr
   \code{"keep"} \tab \verb{great <U+1F600> work} \tab yes \tab nothing \cr
   \code{"shortcode"} \tab \code{great :grinning: work} \tab \strong{yes} \tab nothing \cr
   \code{"name"} \tab \verb{great grinning face work} \tab no \tab the delimiters; the name is now ordinary words \cr
   \code{"placeholder"} \tab \verb{great [emoji] work} \tab no \tab \emph{which} emoji -- the position survives \cr
   \code{"strip"} \tab \verb{great work} \tab no \tab that there was an emoji at all \cr
   any, with \code{hidden = "remove"} \tab the same, less any hidden payload \tab no \tab the payload, by design \cr
}


So if the pipeline has to restore emoji downstream, \code{"shortcode"} is the
only policy that permits it, and it holds up on the awkward cases:
skin-tone modifiers, flags, ZWJ sequences and keycaps all come back.
Measured against the whole catalogue: for all 3790 emoji that have a
shortcode, in their canonical (fully qualified) spelling, which is the
spelling a keyboard emits and text normally holds, \strong{the round trip returns the
original text byte for byte, 100\% of the time}.

\strong{The other 182 have no shortcode, and \code{"shortcode"} leaves them in the
text.} They are the Emoji 17.0 and 18.0 additions: the catalogue knows
them, but the shortcodes come from the \pkg{emoji} package, which does not
yet. They survive the round trip trivially, but a column sanitised with
\code{"shortcode"} can still hold them, so if the point was to keep emoji away
from a model, check with \code{\link[=emoji_summary]{emoji_summary()}} or use \code{"name"}, which reaches
them because they do have names.

\strong{That row of the table assumes the default \code{wrap}.} Restoring the text
means \code{\link[=text_to_emoji]{text_to_emoji()}} can find the token, and it reads exactly
\verb{:shortcode:} -- a colon, the alias, a colon. So \code{wrap} is part of the
reversibility contract, not a cosmetic choice:
\itemize{
\item \code{wrap = ":{x}:"} (the default) restores the original text exactly.
\item A \code{wrap} that decorates the token -- \code{"[:{x}:]"}, \code{":{x}:!"} -- brings the
emoji back but leaves the decoration behind, so the emoji is restored and
the text is not.
\item A \code{wrap} with no \verb{:token:} at all -- \code{"{x}"}, \code{"<{x}>"}, \code{"@{x}@"},
\code{":{x}"} -- restores nothing: the shortcode stays in the text as an
ordinary word. This is the case to watch, because it fails silently.
\item \code{wrap = "::{x}::"} is worse than either: the inner \verb{:grinning:} matches,
so the emoji comes back wrapped in the leftover colons (\verb{:<U+1F600>:}) and
a further round trip keeps adding to them.
}

Change \code{wrap} for readability by all means, but not on a column you intend
to restore.

\strong{The default \code{wrap} also assumes something about the input text, and this is
the one failure mode that choosing it does not remove.} \code{\link[=text_to_emoji]{text_to_emoji()}}
cannot tell a \verb{:shortcode:} token this verb wrote from one the text already
held, so a corpus that arrives with literal shortcode tokens in it gains an
emoji where it had none. That is not a corner case: it is how people type in
the Slack, Discord and GitHub exports the LLM-preprocessing workflow is aimed
at.
\itemize{
\item \code{"nice work :thumbsup:"} sanitises unchanged, and restores as
\code{"nice work "} plus \verb{U+1F44D}. Emoji count 0 before the round trip, 1
after.
\item It compounds with real emoji rather than replacing them:
\code{"nice work :thumbsup:"} followed by \verb{U+1F600} restores with two emoji.
\item The damage is bounded at one pass. A second round trip changes nothing,
because by then the token is a glyph.
}

The colon hazards \code{\link[=text_to_emoji]{text_to_emoji()}} \emph{does} handle are the ones that are not
shortcode-shaped: a clock time (\code{"meet at 10:30"}) and a ratio
(\code{"ratio 3:4"}) both survive untouched, because the token it matches is a
colon, alias characters, and a closing colon. So the contract is precise:
the round trip restores the original text exactly when the only
\verb{:shortcode:}-shaped tokens in it are the ones this verb wrote. If your text
may hold real ones, compare \code{emoji_density()}'s \code{.emoji_n} before and after,
which is the cheapest way to see the inflation.

Unicode also lists shorter spellings of the same emoji, with the \code{U+FE0F}
presentation selectors omitted. Feed one of those in and the round trip
returns the \emph{canonical} spelling instead: \verb{U+270C} comes back as
\verb{U+270C U+FE0F}. Across all 4853 spellings that have a shortcode that is
79.5\% byte-identical, and the remaining 20.5\% differ by \code{U+FE0F} alone,
never by more. The emoji is always the same emoji, and every tidyEmoji lookup
treats the two spellings as one, so this matters only if you are diffing raw
bytes on text that had its selectors stripped upstream.

\code{"placeholder"} keeps \emph{where} but not \emph{which}, which is enough to use "an
emoji was here" as a model feature and not enough to reconstruct the text.
\code{"name"} is the accessibility answer rather than the reversible one -- it
is what a screen reader announces.
